WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
How can an added base regroup every downstream codon?

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.180
The answer lies in how a cell divides a continuous message.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.340
Watch the same ribbon throughout this film.

4
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.940
Its letters will stay fixed until we deliberately change them.

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:21.607
A ribosome needs a starting place.

6
00:00:22.947 --> 00:00:27.767
Once initiation selects that site, a teal reading window locks onto the message.

7
00:00:29.107 --> 00:00:34.007
The window covers a triplet, then advances to the next nonoverlapping triplet.

8
00:00:34.007 --> 00:00:37.027
Each window position is a codon.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.720
The frame is the alignment between that window and the ribbon.

10
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.660
Start at a different base, and every box contains different letters.

11
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.160
Start at the same base, and the boxes remain fixed as the window moves.

12
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.820
A fixed start makes each next triplet a codon.

13
00:00:56.883 --> 00:01:01.563
One base or two bases make too few words for all the amino acids.

14
00:01:03.003 --> 00:01:04.003
Triplets make enough.

15
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:07.663
So fixed-length words must be at least triplets.

16
00:01:08.863 --> 00:01:11.323
Only experiments could test the real reading unit.

17
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:14.697
Now replace a base inside a codon.

18
00:01:15.837 --> 00:01:17.917
The surrounding boundaries do not move.

19
00:01:19.117 --> 00:01:24.977
That codon may keep the same amino acid, specify another amino acid, or become a

20
00:01:24.977 --> 00:01:28.997
stop. Later codons keep their original grouping.

21
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.780
Predict what happens if a base is inserted after the start.

22
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.620
Will only the nearest codon be regrouped, or will every downstream codon be

23
00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.940
regrouped? Hold the reading window still and follow the ribbon.

24
00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:51.783
The new base joins the ribbon.

25
00:01:53.123 --> 00:01:56.683
Every downstream letter slides relative to the fixed window.

26
00:01:58.043 --> 00:02:01.923
New triplets appear all the way along, until a stop ends translation.

27
00:02:03.343 --> 00:02:06.803
This is a frameshift: the grouping changed, not merely a letter.

28
00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:10.857
Researchers called the shift classes plus and minus.

29
00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:16.717
A nearby plus and minus often restored function after a disrupted stretch.

30
00:02:18.057 --> 00:02:22.557
Same-sign shifts did not restore the frame until a complete triplet's worth

31
00:02:22.557 --> 00:02:27.557
accumulated. The later message then returned to its earlier grouping.

32
00:02:28.717 --> 00:02:32.877
In phage experiments, those patterns often restored gene function.

33
00:02:34.137 --> 00:02:37.717
They supported a fixed reading point and a triplet, or a multiple.

34
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.920
Genetics supported a fixed frame, but did not assign the dictionary.

35
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:50.000
Cell-free translation and ribosome-binding tests linked defined RNA messages to

36
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.820
amino acids. Together, the evidence turned moving boxes into codons with

37
00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:56.820
assignments.

38
00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:01.670
A substitution can alter a codon without moving later boundaries.

39
00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:08.150
An insertion or deletion that is not a multiple of three moves the downstream frame.

40
00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:11.010
The lasting lesson is simple.

41
00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:16.030
Keep the starting register, and every later codon keeps its place.
